[^6]: Zone 2 training drives mitochondrial biogenesis via the PGC-1α pathway, raising mitochondrial density and lactate threshold (the "floor"); high-intensity intervals are the direct stimulus for raising the absolute VO2 max "ceiling." See [Data Driven Athlete — Zone 2 guide](https://www.datadrivenathlete.org/blog/zone-2-training-complete-guide) and [Biology Insights — Does Zone 2 improve VO2 max?](https://biologyinsights.com/does-zone-2-training-improve-vo2-max/).

[^7]: The 5-10-5 pro-agility test measures *change-of-direction speed* in a pre-planned pattern rather than true reactive agility, since the athlete isn't responding to an unpredictable stimulus; times ≤4.5 s indicate a good athlete. See [Science for Sport — Pro-Agility Test](https://www.scienceforsport.com/pro-agility-5-10-5-test/) and [Physiopedia](https://www.physio-pedia.com/Pro-Agility_(5-10-5)_Test).

[^8]: The interference effect: resistance training drives hypertrophy via mTOR, endurance training drives adaptation via AMPK, and AMPK suppresses mTOR — so concurrent training can blunt strength/hypertrophy gains, with the effect scaling with endurance intensity. See [Barbell Medicine — Concurrent Training and the Interference Effect](https://www.barbellmedicine.com/blog/concurrent-training-and-the-interference-effect/) and Baar K., "Using Molecular Biology to Maximize Concurrent Training," *Sports Medicine* (2014), [PMC4213370](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4213370/).
